 ],
 "D_um": 720,
 "blurb": "A slice through the clear front window of the human eye, outside at the top. Five layers: a thin skin of cells, a tough sheet, a thick stack of collagen sheets with a few flat cells, a thin membrane, and a single layer of pump cells. Click a layer.",
 "note": "Schematic: the human cornea. A dog's cornea has no distinct Bowman layer, and birds' corneas were not checked; where the dog's cornea leads here, the scene stands in for it. Classic five-layer description. Real sizes: central thickness of the layers (epithelium 52.5, Bowman layer 17.7, stroma 459.1 micrometres, from an optical-coherence-tomography study) Descemet membrane 7 and endothelium 5 micrometres (review article); about 200 collagen lamellae, each up to 0.2 mm broad and 2 micrometres thick; 5-7 epithelial cell layers (5 drawn); basal cell about 20 micrometres tall; surface cells 40-60 micrometres across and 2-6 micrometres thick (5 drawn; the wing cells take up the rest, so their height is illustrative); the surface cells are living and not keratinised; endothelial cell width 17 micrometres is calculated from a birth density of 3,500 cells per square millimetre (adults have fewer, larger cells). Illustrative: the 300 by 100 micrometre window, the cell widths of basal and wing cells, the keratocyte size and number (80 drawn; real keratocytes may be up to 15 percent of stromal volume), the alternating lamella directions, and the gaps between lamellae (drawn about 2.6 micrometres wide; in life the sheets lie in contact with thin ground substance and cells). The collagen fibrils inside a lamella (about 31 nanometres wide) are far too thin to draw at this scale. Layer sizes sum to a little more than the measured total because the sources differ (layers 541 against a measured 529 micrometres).",
 "labels": [
